- How would you test concurrency and network partitions affecting reconciliation?
- How would you prune historical data without breaking causality remediation?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates design of a scalable, non-destructive editing pipeline, leveraging Swift concurrency, Core Image, and memory-aware rendering. Also tests undo/redo, testability, and accessibility considerations in a production-like feature.

## Key Concepts
- Swift concurrency and actors for safe state sharing
- Core Image for GPU-accelerated filters
- Non-destructive editing via composable filter passes
- Undo/redo through graph state snapshots
- Memory management with tile-based rendering and lazy recomputation
- Testability: unit tests for graph evaluation; integration tests for export
- Accessibility hooks for UI feedback

## Code Example
```swift
// Skeleton: Filter protocol and graph actor
actor FilterGraph {
  struct Node { var id: String; var filter: CIFilter }
  var nodes: [Node] = []
  func applyAll(base: CIImage) async -> CIImage {
    var current = base
    for n in nodes {
      n.filter.setValue(current, forKey: kCIInputImageKey)
      if let out = n.filter.outputImage {
        current = out
      }
    }
    return current
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to handle very large images on memory-constrained devices?
- What trade-offs exist between tile-based rendering vs full-image compositing, and how would you test them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of combining UI state with asynchronous persistence, a common real-world pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Combine debouncing for UI-driven writes
- File I/O and JSON encoding in Swift
- SwiftUI data flow and @Published bindings
- Unit testing asynchronous code and filesystem writes

## Code Example
```swift
import Combine
import SwiftUI

class NotesStore: ObservableObject {
  @Published var note: String = ""
  private var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()
  init() {
    $note
      .debounce(for: .milliseconds(500), scheduler: RunLoop.main)
      .sink { [weak self] text in
        self?.save(text)
      }
      .store(in: &cancellables)
  }
  func save(_ text: String) {
    let json = ["note": text]
    if let data = try? JSONSerialization.data(withJSONObject: json, options: []) {
      let url = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
        .appendingPathComponent("notes.json")
      try? data.write(to: url)
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cancellation of a pending save when the app moves to background?
- How would you extend to multi-note scenarios with conflict resolution?
